    {
      "id": "g02",
      "title": {
        "t": "haben: ich habe, du hast …",
        "s": "The verb \"to have\""
      },
      "explain": "**haben** means *to have*. It is almost regular – only **du hast** and **er/sie/es hat** are special.\n**wir** means *we*. With **wir**, the verb looks just like the **Sie** form: **wir haben**, **wir wohnen**, **wir kommen**.\nTip: after **haben**, a der-noun takes **einen**: Ich habe **einen** Bruder. With die- and das-nouns nothing changes: Ich habe **eine** Schwester, ich habe **ein** Kind. You'll learn more about this in Unit 5.",
      "table": [
        [
          "ich",
          "habe",
          "Ich habe zwei Kinder."
        ],
        [
          "du",
          "hast",
          "Hast du Geschwister?"
        ],
        [
          "er / sie / es",
          "hat",
          "Anna hat zwei Brüder."
        ],
        [
          "wir",
          "haben",
          "Wir haben ein Baby."
        ],
        [
          "Sie",
          "haben",
          "Haben Sie Kinder?"
        ]
      ],
